    "https://alfreduniversity.applytojob.com/apply/x50Dwt7pQh/Preparator-And-Exhibitions-Project-Manager?source=Our Career Page Widget": {
      "title": "Preparator and Exhibitions Project Manager",
      "description": "Preparator and Exhibitions Project Manager - Alfred University - Career Page Skip To Job Description View All Jobs View Our Website Thanks for visiting our Career Page. Please review our open positions and apply to the positions that match your qualifications. To learn more about how Alfred University is Outside of Ordinary, please visit our website. If you need assistance, please contact Tamara Green, by emailing [email protected] or calling 607-871-2965. Preparator and Exhibitions Project Manager Alfred, NY Full Time Museum of Ceramic Art Experienced Share The Preparator and Exhibitions Project Manager serves as the operational project manager for museum exhibitions annually, each typically involving artwork with an aggregate insured value exceeding $400,000. The position is responsible for coordinating the planning, preparation, installation, deinstallation and documentation of exhibitions. Working closely with the Museum Director and Curator of Collections, the position supports the curator’s exhibition plan and manages the operational activities necessary to complete exhibitions safely, professionally and on schedule. Long-term priorities are established in collaboration with the Museum Director. Within those priorities, the position independently organizes daily work, coordinates resources and exercises professional judgment in areas such as art handling, installation methods, exhibition lighting, equipment use, object protection and workplace safety. The Museum Director retains ﬁnal authority for exhibition decisions  but works in close consultation with the Project Manager to ensure exhibitions are on time, on budget, properly promoted and meet professional museum standards. Rate: $26.93-$28.48, 35hrs/week Essential Functions: Serve as the operational project manager for museum exhibitions, each typically involving artwork with an aggregate insured value exceeding $400,000. Coordinate exhibition schedules, workﬂow calendars, installation and deinstallation activities, staﬃng, facilities support, transportation, equipment, and required materials. Work with the Museum Director to establish long-term exhibition priorities and independently organize daily work to support those priorities. Support the curator’s exhibition plan and coordinate the operational activities necessary for successful implementation. Collaborate with the Museum Director, Curator of Collections, artists, exhibitors, faculty, staﬀ, vendors, and campus partners. Identify scheduling, operational, safety, security, and object-preservation risks and communicate concerns or potential delays promptly. Provide regular project updates to the Museum Director. Support the timely completion and opening of exhibitions, with ﬁnal accountability retained by the Museum Director. Exhibition Preparation and Art Handling: Install and deinstall high-value artwork and exhibition materials using professional museum practices. Pack, unpack, move, arrange, store, and transport museum objects and incoming or outgoing loans. Prepare gallery spaces and museum infrastructure for exhibitions. Assemble, maintain, and dismantle exhibition furniture, display structures, plinths, wall text, and related materials. Install and adjust exhibition lighting according to accepted museum standards. Install and support multimedia equipment, including monitors, projectors, and related technology. Create and maintain exhibition work lists for insurance, logistics, and documentation purposes. Assist with condition reports for incoming and outgoing objects. Exercise independent technical judgment regarding art handling, installation methods, equipment use, lighting, and object protection. Pause or stop work when a safety, security, or object-preservation concern is identiﬁed. Collections and Storage Support: Oversee the organization and maintenance of the museum’s oﬀ-site storage space in collaboration with the Curator of Collections. Assist with collection-management activities, artwork movement, storage planning, and object documentation. Help ensure that stored artwork and museum equipment are appropriately arranged, protected, and accessible. Maintain gallery, storage, preparation, and work areas in a safe, secure, and organized condition. Maintain an inventory of exhibition tools, equipment, furniture, materials, and supplies. Support compliance with insurance requirements and loan agreements involving national and international lenders. Temporary Staff and Volunteer Coordination: Recruit and hire temporary student employees and temporary art handlers based on exhibition needs and established University procedures. Train, schedule, supervise, and direct temporary workers. Provide instruction in professional art handling, equipment use, safety procedures, and assigned exhibition responsibilities. Monitor work to ensure tasks are completed safely and according to museum standards. Coordinate temporary assignments that may range from one day to several weeks. Provide performance feedback and address work concerns involving temporary workers and volunteers as appropriate. Vendor, Facilities, and Purchasing Coordination: Obtain competitive quotes or vendor proposals for exhibition-related services, transportation, materials, equipment, and other project needs. Present vendor information and recommendations to the Museum Director for review and approval. Coordinate approved vendors and contractors and monitor their work through completion. Work with University Facilities staﬀ on gallery modiﬁcations, exhibition installations, equipment needs, and specialized projects. Serve as a primary museum contact for Facilities and Public Safety regarding exhibition and building needs. Initiate purchases within approved exhibition plans and established University purchasing procedures. Track exhibition-related purchases and provide documentation to the Museum Director. Coordinate the rental of appropriate vehicles and transportation of artwork as needed. Operate within approved budgets without independent budgetary control, vendor-selection authority, or contract-signing authority. Exhibition Communication and Documentation: Maintain organized exhibition records, including schedules, contracts, work lists, correspondence, images, catalogs, publicity materials, and installation documentation. Coordinate with artists and exhibitors to obtain images, statements, biographies, curricula vitae, and other supporting materials. Assist with gathering and organizing information for exhibition labels, wall text, and related interpretive materials. Coordinate museum photography and professional exhibition documentation, including photography, video, and virtual tours. Work with appropriate University staﬀ or external partners to support advertising, press releases, graphic design, and exhibition publicity. Assist with artist visits, lectures, special programs, and other exhibition-related events. Ensure that exhibition ﬁles are complete, accurate, and maintained according to museum and University practices. Communicate project requirements, deadlines, responsibilities, and changes to appropriate stakeholders. Other Duties and Responsibilities: Supports and assists in creating intersections and pathways for faculty, staﬀ and students that will assist in transforming people’s lives. Supports and assists in creating an evolving culture of connection, mentoring and rich engagement opportunities for faculty, staﬀ, and students. Maintains a commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion and belonging, and a high degree of cultural competence and respect for a wide range of identities and experiences, therefore welcoming and supporting all faculty, staﬀ and students and expanding access to the AU experience. Maintains a safe working environment within and around their facilities and associated equipment and supplies including, but not limited to, obtaining Safety Data Sheet (SDS) information pertaining to any hazards associated with their work environment. Participates in all required assigned training, including safety training and will comply with safety rules, regulations, and protocols. Contributes to the overall success of the University by performing other duties as assigned. Qualifications-Education & Experiencem, Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: Minimum of a Bachelor’s degree in museum studies, ﬁne arts, art history or related ﬁeld is required. Master’s degree preferred Minimum of 3 years of professional museum or gallery experience is required, professional museum experience is preferred. Demonstrated experience with professional art handling, exhibition installation and deinstallation, and the safe packing, movement, storage, and transportation of artwork. Experience coordinating complex, deadline-driven exhibition projects involving multiple stakeholders, resources, and priorities. Knowledge of professional museum practices related to exhibition preparation, object protection, storage, safety, accessibility, and documentation. Experience with exhibition logistics, including scheduling, shipping, installation, vendor coordination, and recordkeeping. Ability to interpret exhibition plans, layouts, and measurements and translate them into completed gallery installations. Ability to identify operational, safety, and object-preservation concerns and develop practical solutions. Experience with basic carpentry and the safe use of hand tools, power tools, ladders, lifts, and related exhibition equipment. Knowledge of exhibition lighting and familiarity with multimedia equipment, including monitors and projectors. Ability to train, supervise, and direct temporary employees, student workers, art handlers, and volunteers. Strong project-management, organizational, problem-solving, and time-management skills, with the ability to manage multiple exhibitions and priorities simultaneously. Strong written and verbal communication skills and the ability to work eﬀectively with artists, curators, students, faculty, staﬀ, vendors, contractors, visitors, and community members. Experience working with ceramic, three-dimensional, fragile, or high-value artwork is preferred. Knowledge of ceramic art, contemporary art, and general art history is preferred. Experience with exhibition design, collections management, condition reporting, museum storage, or work within an educational environment is preferred. Experience obtaining competitive quotes or vendor proposals and coordinating approved vendors or contractors is preferred. Proﬁciency with Microsoft Oﬃce is required. Experience with Adobe InDesign, FileMaker Pro, SketchUp, AutoCAD, or comparable design and database software is preferred. Ability to work occasional evenings or weekends during exhibition installation, deinstallation, and special programming. A valid driver’s license is required. Physical Demands & Work Environment: The physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an employee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodation may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform essential functions. Personal Protective Equipment must be worn when required.  A respiratory function test and the ability to use respirators may be required.  Stairs and uneven surfaces may be present; artificial lifts, etc., may or may not be available.  The position may require the incumbent to walk outdoors in all types of weather to other buildings or offices located on the campus.  The Campus is in an area that contains numerous hills and slopes and may not always be fully accessible for mobility-impaired individuals. About Alfred University: Lighting the way for students since 1836. “We've always been a place for makers, especially those who make the future. Our commitment to independent thinking, creativity, academic rigor, and community have made us a unique kind of leader since 1836.” Alfred University (AU) was founded on principles of social justice in 1836 by liberal, independent thinkers who placed high value on education for all citizens. The University has retained and built upon the strong values of its founders, developing as an institution of national and international renown that is responsive to the needs of contemporary society while remaining consistent with the spirit of its origins. Alfred University is the second oldest coeducational college in the United States as well as one of the earliest nineteenth-century colleges to have enrolled African American and Native American students. It has a long-standing history of educating socially conscious students who make a difference in their professions and their communities. Over the course of the twentieth century, Alfred University evolved into a complex institution offering a full range of programs in the liberal arts and sciences, art and design, engineering, business, education, counseling, and school psychology to its nearly 2,000 undergraduate and graduate students that live, learn, and play on a 232-acre campus located in an idyllic, creative valley in Western New York. Alfred University actively subscribes to a policy of equal employment opportunity, and will not discriminate against any employee, student or applicant because of race, age, sex, color, sexual orientation, gender identification or expression, physical or mental disability, religion, ancestry or national origin, marital status, genetic information, military or veteran status, domestic violence victim status, criminal conviction status, political affiliation or any other characteristic protected by applicable law. Share Apply Apply for this position Required * First Name * Last Name * Email Address * Phone * Address * Resume * We've received your resume. Click here to update it. Attach resume or Paste resume Attach resume as .pdf, .doc, .docx, .odt, .txt, or .rtf (limit 5MB) or Paste resume Paste your resume here or Attach resume file Cover Letter * References: Please enter names and contact information: * Are you related to anyone currently working at Alfred University? If so, please state who: * Would you require a VISA sponsorship to work for our University? * -- No answer -- Yes No Are you an External or Internal Candidate? * -- No answer -- External Internal The following questions are entirely optional. To comply with government Equal Employment Opportunity and/or Affirmative Action reporting regulations, we are requesting (but NOT requiring) that you enter this personal data. This information will not be used in connection with any employment decisions, and will be used solely as permitted by state and federal law. Your voluntary cooperation would be appreciated. Learn more . Gender Decline to answer Female Male Race/Ethnicity Decline to answer Hispanic or Latino White, not Hispanic or Latino Black or African-American, not Hispanic or Latino Asian, not Hispanic or Latino Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander, not Hispanic or Latino American Indian or Alaskan Native, not Hispanic or Latino Two or More Races, not Hispanic or Latino MENA (Middle Eastern and North African) Invitation for Job Applicants to Self-Identify as a U.S. Veteran A “disabled veteran” is one of the following: a veteran of the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service who is entitled to compensation (or who but for the receipt of military retired pay would be entitled to compensation) under laws administered by the Secretary of Veterans Affairs; or a person who was discharged or released from active duty because of a service-connected disability. A “recently separated veteran” means any veteran during the three-year period beginning on the date of such veteran's discharge or release from active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval, or air service. An “active duty wartime or campaign badge veteran” means a veteran who served on active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service during a war, or in a campaign or expedition for which a campaign badge has been authorized under the laws administered by the Department of Defense. An “Armed forces service medal veteran” means a veteran who, while serving on active duty in the U.S. military, ground, naval or air service, participated in a United States military operation for which an Armed Forces service medal was awarded pursuant to Executive Order 12985. Veteran status I IDENTIFY AS ONE OR MORE OF THE CLASSIFICATIONS OF PROTECTED VETERAN LISTED ABOVE I AM NOT A PROTECTED VETERAN I DON’T WISH TO ANSWER Voluntary Self-Identification of Disability Voluntary Self-Identification of Disability Form CC-305 OMB Control Number 1250-0005 Expires 07/31/2029 Why are you being asked to complete this form? We are a federal contractor or subcontractor. The law requires us to provide equal employment opportunity to qualified\n                                            people with disabilities. We have a goal of having at least 7% of our workers as people with disabilities. The law says we\n                                            must measure our progress towards this goal. To do this, we must ask applicants and employees if they have a disability\n                                            or have ever had one. People can become disabled, so we need to ask this question at least every five years. Completing this form is voluntary, and we hope that you will choose to do so. Your answer is confidential. No one who\n                                            makes hiring decisions will see it. Your decision to complete the form and your answer will not harm you in any way. If you\n                                            want to learn more about the law or this form, visit the U.S. Department of Labor’s Office of Federal Contract Compliance\n                                            Programs (OFCCP) website at www.dol.gov/ofccp . How do you know if you have a disability? A disability is a condition that substantially limits one or more of your “major life activities.” If you have or have ever had\n                                            such a condition, you are a person with a disability. Disabilities include, but are not limited to: Alcohol or other substance use\n                                                disorder (not currently using\n                                                drugs illegally) Autoimmune disorder, for\n                                                example, lupus, fibromyalgia,\n                                                rheumatoid arthritis, HIV/AIDS Blind or low vision Cancer (past or present) Cardiovascular or heart\n                                                disease Celiac disease Cerebral palsy Deaf or serious difficulty\n                                                hearing Diabetes Disfigurement, for example,\n                                                disfigurement caused by burns,\n                                                wounds, accidents, or congenital\n                                                disorders Epilepsy or other seizure disorder Gastrointestinal disorders, for example,\n                                                Crohn's Disease, irritable bowel\n                                                syndrome Intellectual or developmental disability Mental health conditions, for example,\n                                                depression, bipolar disorder, anxiety\n                                                disorder, schizophrenia, PTSD Missing limbs or partially missing limbs Mobility impairment, benefiting from the\n                                                use of a wheelchair, scooter, walker,\n                                                leg brace(s) and/or other supports Nervous system condition, for example,\n                                                migraine headaches, Parkinson’s\n                                                disease, multiple sclerosis (MS) Neurodivergence, for example,\n                                                attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder\n                                                (ADHD), autism spectrum disorder,\n                                                dyslexia, dyspraxia, other learning\n                                                disabilities Partial or complete paralysis (any\n                                                cause) Pulmonary or respiratory conditions, for\n                                                example, tuberculosis, asthma,\n                                                emphysema Short stature (dwarfism) Traumatic brain injury Please check one of the boxes below: YES, I HAVE A DISABILITY, OR HAVE HAD ONE IN THE PAST NO, I DO NOT HAVE A DISABILITY AND HAVE NOT HAD ONE IN THE PAST I DO NOT WANT TO ANSWER PUBLIC BURDEN STATEMENT: According to the Paperwork Reduction Act of 1995 no persons are required to respond to a collection of information unless such collection displays a valid OMB control number. This survey should take about 5 minutes to complete. You must enter your name and date Name Date Human Check * Submit Application This website uses cookies and other analytics technologies. By selecting \"Allow\", you consent to the recording, use and sharing of your website activity by this website and its service providers. Allow Reject All Powered by"
